	('9e4c5c97-9e79-427a-ad7e-65faac5f1fe4', '89f29d7c-723f-4203-9349-d81228744f3d', 'Machine Shop Quality Inspector', 'The Quality Control Inspector - will work at the Erlanger, KY Service Center to inspect and verify large-scale, power generation equipment and their subassemblies for compliance with engineering specifications, blueprints, and quality standards.
Key Responsibilities & Duties
Dimensional Verification:
Perform comprehensive first-piece, in-process, and final inspections on complex, large-scale machined components.
Blueprint Interpretation:
Expertly analyze and interpret complex engineering drawings, including Geometric Dimensioning and Tolerancing (GD&T) specifications, to verify part conformity.
Non-Conformance Reporting:
Identify and formally document non-conforming parts. Collaborate with shop floor personnel to quarantine affected material and participate in root cause investigations to prevent recurrence.
Documentation Excellence:
Maintain inspection logs, complete First Article Inspection (FAI) reports, and ensure all quality documentation supports full material traceability.
Tool Calibration Oversight:
Conduct regular audits of inspection equipment to ensure all tools are calibrated, operational, and stored correctly.
Continuous Improvement:
Partner with CNC Machinists to monitor manufacturing processes, identifying potential quality risks early in the production cycle to minimize scrap and rework.
Safety Adherence:
Maintain a high standard of shop floor safety, ensuring all inspection activities are performed in accordance with site-specific safety protocols and PPE requirements.
Technical Proficiency Requirements
The candidate must demonstrate advanced proficiency in the setup and use of the following manual precision measuring instruments:
Micrometers:
Precision measurement using outside, inside, depth, and thread micrometers.
Gage Blocks:
Building and assembling gage blocks accurately for checking gages to a standard.
Calipers:
Competent use of vernier, dial, and digital calipers for high-accuracy external, internal, and depth measurements.
Indicators:
Use of dial test indicators and magnetic bases for checking runout, parallelism, perpendicularity, and flatness on surface plates.
Height Gages:
Utilization of vernier and digital height gages for layout, vertical dimension verification, and surface plate setups.
Fixed Gages:
Proficiency with go/no-go plug gages, thread plug gages, and ring gages to verify hole and thread fitment.
Bore Gages:
Precise internal diameter measurement of cylindrical and bearing surfaces.
Angular Measurement:
Use of adjustable protractor heads, precision angle blocks, and sine bars for verifying tapers and angular tolerances.', NULL, 'Proven experience as a Quality Inspector in a machine shop environment.
Deep understanding of manual precision measurement techniques.
Ability to read and interpret complex technical blueprints.
Strong attention to detail with the ability to maintain clear, accurate, and legible handwritten documentation.
Physical ability to maneuver around large-scale power generation components and perform inspections in various shop floor positions.
Pay Transparency:
The pay for this position is at $35.00/hour. This posting is expected to close when a suitable candidate has been identified.
*The Company pays a geographic differential of 110%, 120% or 130% of salary in certain areas.
